Composition is another key component of any musical culture. No musical culture can
survive off of merely recycling existing material, even if that material is a
major influence in the culture. This is true in _Stray_ as well. The most
prominent example of composition that the player encounters in _Stray_ is three
break dancing robots, named Jeanma, Miko, and Simon, in an apartment complex
within the Midtown level. When first interacting with these characters, they
lament the recently installed security cameras which prevent them from
performing their dance routine in the complex @gameplay[4:12:42-4:16:55]. The player can then knock down
these cameras and the robots will begin dancing and playing music. Simon will
also reward the player with a cassette tape containing their most recent music.
Their music evokes human and nonhuman elements like much of _Stray_'s other
diegetic music. The music itself is a lofi beat with dial-up tones added in, a
representation of the game's "cozy cyberpunk" aesthetic @cld. The lofi tune
has robotic, synthesized elements and yet it still resembles a relaxing human
genre of music. The composer did this very intentionally. He writes "I tried to
imitate music that humans used to do" when referencing the diegetic
compositions present in game @sn. The music that Jeanma, Miko, and Simon
create isn't just duplicating human music though. The most obvious example of
this is the fact that their music is in opposition to the environment they live
in. If they were mindlessly replicating music that came before it is unlikely
that the security state would be cracking down on them. Another more
interesting example of this takes place shortly after the players encounter
with the robots in the apartment complex. After retrieving the cassette from
Simon, the player can insert the cassette into a boombox in the back of a store
owned by a grumpy robot named Ozi @gameplay[4:17:29]. After inserting the cassette into the
boombox, Ozi will cover his ears and rush to turn off the music, clearly having
a distaste for the music @gameplay[4:18:04]. This is evidence of the same generational music
differences that people are familiar with today @gdmp. The stereotypical
"Music back in my day" attitude appears just as present in the world of _Stray_
as it has even been. This further supports the idea that robots have their own
unique musical culture. The existence of an intergenerational music gap implies
that the musical culture of _Stray_ is alive and well, changing and evolving as
time goes on, rather than a stagnant remnant of the long gone humans. It
changes and evolves so much that some robots think of newly composed music as
the loud, obnoxious music of the next generation. 

Music doesn't always have to be written completely from scratch for it to be
new and innovative. Remixing and sampling is another way that musical culture
can grow and develop. This is true of the musical culture in _Stray_ as well.
The game features numerous examples of musical remixes; a piece will be sped up
or slowed down, parts will be added or removed, or it will be played with
different instrumentation. This is true of Jeanma, Miko, and Simon's music as
well. Their music is remixed in a nightclub that the player encounters at the
end of Midtown. The music in question a remix of the music on the cassette that
the player received earlier. Both the original and the remix are in the
techno/house music style, with a super loud, thumping base and a lot of
synthesized instrumentation @lsu. With its loud music, flashing lights,
dancing holograms, and DJs scratching records @gameplay[4:56:34], the nightclub is clearly trying
to emulate the raves and nightclubs of today. In so doing, the nightclub is
trying to call upon the same cultural associations that house music has in the
modern day to counterculture and the underground @lsu. This is an example of
game music referencing cultural phenomena as a means to make the player
associate that phenomena with the game world @sdam. Returning to the musical
culture of _Stray_, this further suggests a lively, reactive musical culture
rather than a static culture that merely replicates the music humans used to
make. In other words, Jeanma, Miko, and Simon make techno not because the
humans before them made techno, but because they live in an environment that
breeds counterculture. Had they lived under different circumstances, their
music would have been different. It's also worth noting the association between
house music/rave culture and transhumanism @lsu. The robots making
transhumanist music suggests that their musical culture is in some part
influenced by their identity as robots. All of these factors combine to make
music influenced by identity, history, and environment. That music can be
considered part of a broader musical culture which is influenced by those same
factors.
